---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import About from "../components/About.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, "/");
---
<Base
  title="mbem"
  description="Mollified boundary element method for 3-D linear elasticity: non-singular displacements and stresses, including on the fault."
  current="home"
>
  <Fragment slot="sidebar">
    <h1>mbem<span class="sub">mollified boundary element method</span></h1>
    <About />
  </Fragment>

  <div
    id="hero"
    class="hero"
    aria-label="Three slices through the maximum shear stress of a 400 by 400 by 200 km elastic box containing a strike-slip fault, a soft cylindrical inclusion and a topographic hill"
  ></div>
  <p class="cap">
    Elastic maximum shear stress through a 400&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;400&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;200&nbsp;km
    box, from a stored solve &mdash; 24,348 unknowns, read out on a 79,000-point
    grid. The box holds a surface-breaking strike-slip fault (<b>pink</b>), a
    cylindrical inclusion ten times softer than its host (<b>blue</b>), and a
    2&nbsp;km Gaussian hill on the free surface, which the fault mesh is warped to
    follow. Drag to orbit. The eigenstress of every mollified jump has been
    subtracted, so this is the <i>elastic</i> stress, and every element is shown.
    See <a href={`${base}concepts/`}>Concepts</a> for
    what that means and <a href={`${base}examples/`}>Examples</a> to move the
    slices yourself.
  </p>

  <style>
    .hero { min-height: 420px; width: 100%; overflow: hidden; cursor: grab; }
    .hero canvas { display: block; }
    .hero:active { cursor: grabbing; }
    .cap { font-size: 13px; color: var(--fg-dim); margin-top: 10px; }
    .cap b { color: var(--fg); font-weight: 600; }
  </style>
  <script>
    import { startHero } from "../viz/hero";
    startHero(document.getElementById("hero")!, import.meta.env.BASE_URL);
  </script>
</Base>
